[{"page":1,"text":"DRAFT\n\nVINEYARD CITY, UTAH\n\nORDINANCE NO. 2026-11\n\nAN ORDINANCE OF THE CITY OF VINEYARD CITY, UTAH, AMENDING THE\n\nVINEYARD ZONING CODE SECTION 15.12.050 DISTRICT USE TABLE, SECTION\n\n15.30.010 PURPOSE, SECTION 15.32.280 CLEAR VIEW TRIANGLE ON CORNER\n\nLOTS, SECTION 15.34.060 ACCESSORY DWELLING UNITS, SECTION 15.38.030\n\nPARKING REQUIREMENTS, AND SECTION 15.60 DEFINITIONS, AND AMENDING\n\nSECTION 1.08.040(S) INTERSECTION DESIGN OF THE VINEYARD\n\nCONSTRUCTION AND DESIGN SPECIFICATIONS, TO CONFORM THE CITY’S\n\nACCESSORY DWELLING UNIT REGULATIONS TO UTAH CODE §§ 10-20-618 AND\n\n10-21-304.\n\nWHEREAS, Vineyard is authorized to amend the City Zoning Ordinance pursuant to\n\nUtah Code Title 10, Chapter 20, Municipal Land Use, Development, and Management\n\nAct; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Utah Legislature enacted Senate Bill 284, Local Land and Water\nModifications, during the 2026 General Session, which the Governor signed on March\n\n18, 2026, and which enacts Utah Code § 10-21-304, Detached accessory dwelling\nunits, effective October 1, 2026; and\nWHEREAS, Vineyard City is a “specified municipality” as that term is defined in Utah\n\nCode § 10-21-101, and is therefore subject to Utah Code § 10-21-304; and\n\nWHEREAS, Utah Code § 10-21-304(1)(a) requires a specified municipality to adopt a\nland use regulation that permits a detached accessory dwelling unit (DADU) on any lot\nor parcel that is 11,000 square feet or larger and contains a single-family dwelling, if the\nsingle-family dwelling is a permitted use on the lot or parcel; and\n\nWHEREAS, the City’s existing accessory dwelling unit (ADU) regulations at VZC\n15.34.060 require a minimum lot size of twelve thousand (12,000) square feet for a\ndetached accessory dwelling unit (DADU), contain no process for the conversion of a\nlegally constructed accessory structure to a DADU, and contain design and parking\nprovisions that require revision to conform to Utah Code §§ 10-21-304 and 10-20-618;\nand\n\nWHEREAS, the Planning Commission held a public hearing on September 2, 2026\nand, after fully considering public comments and staff recommendations, recommended\napproval to the Vineyard City Council; and","ocr":false},{"page":2,"text":"DRAFT\n\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council, having reviewed the proposed text amendment,\nheld a public hearing on ______________, 2026; and\n\nWHEREAS, the Vineyard City Council, having considered the recommendation of the\nPlanning Commission and the comments submitted by the public, has determined that it\nis in the best interest of the public health, safety, and welfare to adopt the proposed text\namendment to the zoning ordinance;\nNOW, THEREFORE, be it ordained by the City Council of Vineyard City, in the State of\n\nUtah, as follows:\nSection 1: Amendment of Section 15.60 “Definitions” of the Vineyard Zoning Code. The\ndefinition of “Dwelling, Accessory Unit” is amended, and the definitions of “Accessory\n\nDwelling Unit, Detached” and “Accessory Dwelling Unit, Internal” are enacted, as\nfollows:\n\nDwelling, Accessory Unit: An Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU) provides\ncomplete, independent living facilities with a separate dwelling entrance than the\nprinciple dwelling. An internal ADU is an attached dwelling unit to a single-family\nhome. A detached ADU (DADU) is a single-family dwelling unit which is located\non the same lot as a primary single-family home. A DADU is designed to be\nclearly subordinate to the principle dwelling.\n\nSection 2: Section 15.12.050 “District Use Table” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is\namended as follows:\n(1) In the District Use Table, under RESIDENTIAL, the use “Dwelling, SingleFamily” needs to be designated a Non-Permitted use in the Regional Mixed-Use\n(RMU) District.\n(2) Add the following items:\n\n5. Accessory Dwelling Units: In any zoning district established under VZC\n\n15.14, Special Purpose Zoning Districts, in which a single-family dwelling\nis a permitted use, a DADU is a permitted use on any lot or parcel that\nqualifies under VZC 15.34.060, notwithstanding the omission of that use\nfrom the district’s use table.\n\n6. As per UC § 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not be\nrequired for a DADU located in a zoning district that is primarily residential.\nSection 3: Section 15.34.060 “Accessory Dwelling Units” of the Vineyard Zoning Code\nis amended as follows:","ocr":false},{"page":3,"text":"DRAFT\n\nPurpose: The purpose of the ADU ordinance is to promote areas in the city to\naccommodate an expanding population, establish regulations to address the health,\nsafety and welfare of the community, to provide flexibility for changes in household size\nassociated with life cycle and to enable a level of financial security for home owners and\nto implement Utah Code § 10-21-304. It applies to both internal ADUs and detached\n\nADUs (DADUs).\n1. Location: Accessory dwelling units are allowed as an accessory use to a singlefamily detached residence and are limited to the following development\nconfigurations:\na. Within a detached accessory structures in accordance with section\n\n15.12.060 Dimensional Standards Table and Section 15.34.030\n\nAccessory Buildings.\nb. Within a single family home provided access to the accessory dwelling\nunit shall meet all applicable fire and building codes.\nc. Within prefabricated housing types intended for long-term placement that\nadhere to a permanent foundation and the designed in accordance with\nsubsection 4, External Appearance.\n\nAn ADU shall not be located:\na. Within a public utility easement or other recorded easement; or\nb. In a front-yard area of a lot or parcel.\n\nAccess to an ADU shall meet all applicable fire and building codes. A mobile\nhome, travel trailer, boat, or similar recreational vehicle shall not be used as an\n\nADU. See VZC 15.34.100, Parking Requirements Of Private Recreational\n\nVehicles In Residential Zoning Districts.\n\n2. Minimum lot size: A minimum lot size of eleven thousand (11,000) square\nfeet is required for lots containing a DADU.\n\n3. Detached accessory dwelling unit size: A DADU shall not exceed the\nsquare footage of the primary residence located on the same lot or parcel;\n\n4. External appearance: The architectural style, building materials and building\ncolors of an accessory dwelling unit shall be compatible and consistent with the\narchitectural style, materials, and color of the primary building.\n\n9. Utilities: An ADU shall be served by, the same water, sewer, electrical, and\ngas meters that serve the primary building. No separate utility lines, connections,\nor meters shall be allowed for an accessory dwelling unit.\n\nA DADU will not be allowed unless it has adequate inside access to sanitary\nsewer, culinary water, storm water, and electricity and there is sufficient capacity\nin each of these utilities.","ocr":false},{"page":4,"text":"DRAFT\n\nAll ADUs shall conform to the following standards:\na. An ADU shall contain complete cooking and bathroom facilities that are\nseparate from the facilities located in the primary dwelling.\ni. The cooking facility in the ADU shall contain:\n(1) A sink and water faucet;\n(2) Capacity for food refrigeration; and\n(3) A permanent, built-in stove top, range, or other similar\ndevice for cooking food.\nii. The bathroom facility in the ADU shall contain:\n(1) A sink and water faucet;\n(2) A toilet; and\n(3) A shower or bathtub.\n\n10. Parking: A single-family dwelling with an internal ADU shall provide a\nminimum of one onsite parking space additional to existing requirements for the\nprimary dwelling with a maximum of four (4) total parking spaces required for\nboth the primary home and ADU together.\n\nA single-family dwelling with a DADU that is less than 650 square feet shall\nprovide a minimum of one (1) additional onsite parking space. A single-family\ndwelling with a DADU that is greater than 650 square feet shall provide a\nminimum of two (2) additional onsite parking spaces.\n\nWhen a detached garage is converted into a DADU, any necessary parking\nspaces from that garage shall be replaced on-site.\n\nParking spaces shall be located on a paved surface or approved driveway. Only\ndriveways or parking pads containing a minimum length of eighteen feet (18’)\nmay be counted toward required onsite parking. The minimum length of a\ndriveway counting toward onsite parking shall not include any land associated\nwith street rights-of-way, sidewalks or public property/access. All onsite parking\nspaces shall contain a minimum width of eight feet (8’). Primary dwelling parking\nshall not be parked tandem with tenant parking and tenant parking shall not be\nparked tandem with primary dwelling parking. The area dedicated to onsite\nparking and driveway surfaces shall be limited in compliance with VZC Section\n\n15.38.030(2)(b).\n\n12. Entrance: Entrances and access to the accessory dwelling unit shall meet\napplicable building and fire codes. Only one (1) front entrance of the primary\nhome shall be located on the front of the primary dwelling.\n\n18. Conversion of Legal Accessory Building to a DADU: The owner of a\nlegally constructed accessory structure may convert that structure to a DADU","ocr":false},{"page":5,"text":"DRAFT\nsubject to the dwelling and accessory structure setback requirements applicable\nto the lot or parcel; and all applicable building, health, and fire codes.\n\n19. As per UC § 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not be required\nfor a DADU located in a zoning district that is primarily residential.\n\nSection 4: Section 15.30.010 “Purpose” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is amended as\nfollows:\nThe purpose of the issuance of a conditional use application is to allow the proper\nintegration of uses that may be suitable only if such uses are designed, arranged, or\nconducted in a particular manner. This chapter identifies and provides the procedures\nfor the review of conditional use applications (identified as “C” Uses) in the table of\nuses.\nAs per UC § 10-21-304 (3)(a) a conditional use permit shall not be required for a DADU\nlocated in a zoning district that is primarily residential.\n\nSection 5: Section 15.32.280 “Clear View Triangle On Corner Lots” of the Vineyard\n\nZoning Code is amended as follows:\n\nWhere two local residential roads intersect within a planned subdivision, no view\nobstruction including a sight-obscuring fence, wall, sign, other similar structures, and\nlandscaping which exceeds two (2) feet in height shall be placed on any corner lot\nwithin a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the curb\nline thirty (30) feet from the projected intersection of such curb lines. Where no curb\nexists, the clear view area shall include that portion of the corner lot lying within a\ntriangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the property line\ntwenty (20) feet from the intersection of said property line. Use the Vineyard City Access\nManagement Manual to design sight triangle dimensions for all other intersections.","ocr":false},{"page":6,"text":"DRAFT\n\nSection 6: Section 1.08.040 (S) “Intersection Design” of the Construction and Design\n\nSpecifications is amended as follows:\n6. Sight Distance Triangle. Where two local residential roads intersect within a planned\nsubdivision, no view obstruction including a sight-obscuring fence, wall, sign, other\nsimilar structures, and landscaping which exceeds two (2) feet in height shall be placed\non any corner lot within a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines\nlocated at the curb line thirty (30) feet from the projected intersection of such curb lines.\nWhere no curb exists, the clear view area shall include that portion of the corner lot lying\nwithin a triangular area formed by a diagonal line connecting lines located at the\nproperty line twenty (20) feet from the intersection of said property line. Use the\nVineyard City Access Management Manual to design sight triangle dimensions for all\nother intersections.","ocr":false},{"page":7,"text":"DRAFT\n\nSection 7: Section 15.38.030 “Parking Requirements” of the Vineyard Zoning Code is\namended as follows:\nOff-street Parking Requirements. The following minimum number of off-street, paved\nparking spaces shall be provided and maintained by ownership, easement and/or lease\nfor and during the life of the respective uses hereinafter set forth. Any proposed uses\nnot listed herein will be determined by the Planner through the site plan approval\nprocess.\n\nTable: Residential Uses — Parking Spaces Required\n\nSingle-family: 3.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nTwo-family: 4.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nThree-family: 4.0 Spaces/Unit\n\nMulti-family: Efficiency/Studio 1.0 Spaces/Unit; 1 Bedroom 1.5 Spaces/Unit; 2\n\nBedrooms 2.0 Spaces/Unit; 3 Bedrooms 3.0 Spaces/Unit. In addition to the required\nspaces, 0.5 guest stalls per unit shall be included.\nSection 8: Severability","ocr":false},{"page":8,"text":"DRAFT\nIf any section, subsection, sentence, clause, or phrase of this Ordinance is held to be\ninvalid or unconstitutional, such decision shall not affect the validity of the remaining\nportions of this Ordinance.\n\nSection 9: Effective Date\nThis Ordinance shall take effect on October 1, 2026, or upon passage and publication\nor posting as required by law, whichever occurs later.\n\nPASSED AND ADOPTED by the Vineyard City Council this _____ day of\n______________ 2026.\n\n___________________________________\n\nZack Stratton\n\nMayor, Vineyard City\n\nSEAL\n\nATTEST:\n\n___________________________________\n\nRobin Raines-Bond\nCity Recorder, Vineyard City","ocr":false},{"page":9,"text":"DRAFT\n\nJacob Holdaway\n\nDavid Lauret\n\nParker McCumber\n\nEzra Nair\n\nJacob Wood\n\nYES\n\nNO\n\nABSENT\n\nABSTAIN\n\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n_____\n\n_______\n_______\n_______\n_______\n_______\n\n________\n________\n________\n______